A||||6||Rodney||New automatic shifter design||||1||
Z||000000||Rodney||01-17-2001||12:31 PM||||I've been working on a new automatic shifter upgrade.<BR>It is mostly designed for those with kit cars that have<BR>the automatic and do not like the look of the automatic<BR>shifter in a car that looks like a Ferrari or such.<P>I have a few variations. It is an assembly that slips<BR>over the original automatic shaft to make it look like<BR>a stick shift. It has a black ball knob and a thin<BR>polished stainless steel tube so it looks like a manual<BR>shifter. To shift you push the shifter down. You can<BR>retain the OEM automatic shift console and show the<BR>indicator. Or I will have a boot to cover the indicator<BR>part. Or you may switch to a manual console top and I<BR>have a boot for that. Switching to the manual shift<BR>console will be the best looking one I feel.<P>When I bought my 88 Mera it was an automatic. I did<BR>change it to a 5 speed. But I was dismayed that there<BR>was no way to disguise it to look like a stick shift.<P>Rodney Dickman<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||01-17-2001||02:18 PM||||Rodney, It sounds interesting. Any Pics? Also some of us like to "grasp the shifter" when we get a little aggressive with driving. Does the shifter just slide over or is it attached some how? I would just be worried that it could just "slip off" if not attached.||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||01-17-2001||03:24 PM||||Sounds cool. Would this shifter also work on a 4T60E tranny like on the 3.8 swaps? Any chance you could make it with aluminum ball on the top? Just spitballin' here...<P>||||reg||14
Z||000003||rockcrawl||01-17-2001||04:16 PM||||I did a similar mod to the auto shifter in my Countach. Shortened the stick, replaced the plastic inner rod with a steel rod welded to the detent at the bottom to keep it from pulling out or turning. Attached a manual type knob to the new steel detent rod. installed gated shift plate. Looks just like a Lambo 5 speed, push down to shift.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||01-17-2001||04:19 PM||||You will be able to just lift the shifter assembly off the post. Nothing holds it down. But you would have to lift about 5 inches to get it off. I could maybe do a real fancy proper shifter but the cost would be many times higher. I doubt I would sell many. Unfortunately that is the trade off on a product with a very limited sales potential. You have to compromise. I know a lot of Ferrari/Lambo kit owners will be happy to have this. When my Mera was an automatic I would have gladly bought this. That auto shifter looked terrible in a car that looks like a Ferrari!!! But I think the stock auto shifters look just fine in a stock Fiero. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||01-18-2001||01:43 AM||||Maybe you could put some kind of twist-n-lock thing at the bottom of the shaft. Kind of like the way you have to twist and lock light bulbs in on  a car. I guess the hard part would be putting the pin on the bottom of the actual shifter. Maybe you could flange out the bottom of the shaft a little and somehow make a pin assembly that just clamps on...<P>Sounds like a great idea but I don't think I would like it sliding up and down either... If you decide to go farther with it, I am interested!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||martin1875||01-18-2001||08:37 PM||||i to didn't like the stock auto shift handle, so i got one of of a berreta in the salvage yard(don't know the year but could check) it looks like a manuel shift knob with a button on the top that you push down. if you covered it with leather or cloth you couldn't till it was a auto shift handle. i left mine like i found it on the berreta||||reg||1
